\begin{document}

\title{Changepoint detection in network measurements}

\author{Allen B. Downey}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}

I have developed a framework for identifying candidate
changepoints in a time series and estimating the probability
that each candidate is a true changepoint.  This framework is
useful for a variety of applications in network measurement and
prediction.  I am seeking support for ongoing work I am planning
for Summer 2006.

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label {intro}

Predicting network round trip times is useful for a variety of
applications.  At the protocol level, these predictions are used to
estimate parameters like timeouts and network capacity
\cite{noureddine02transmission}.  In middleware, they are used for
resource selection and scheduling \cite{wolski99network} And at the
application level, they are used for predicting transfer times and
choosing among mirror sites.

Most practical models of network performance are stationary, but
measurements in the current Internet show that network performance
is characterized by periods of stationarity punctuated with
abrupt transitions.  On short time scales, these transitions are
caused by variations in traffic.  On medium time scales, they are
caused by routing changes and transient hardware changes (e.g. routers
and links going down and coming back up).  On longer time scales they
are caused by hardware changes like upgrades and new
hardware.

I believe that detection of abrupt changes in time series
data is an important problem for network measurement and modeling.

Statisticians have done extensive work on the general problem of
changepoint detection, but this work has not yet crossed over into
computer science.  The two goals of my project are to develop a novel
technique for the general problem of changepoint detection and to
pioneer the application of these techniques to applications in
computer science.


\subsection{Changepoint detection}

Two general approaches to changepoint detection are online and
offline.  Online techniques are incremental; they deal with incoming
data as it arrives; offline techniques can only run after all data are
available.  This distinction is partly misleading, though, since it is
possible to run an ``offline'' algorithm every time a new point
arrives.  But offline algorithms are often too
computationally-intensive to be practical for real-time processing.
Also, the goal of offline processing is usually to identify every
changepoint in a sequence; most online algorithms are only interested
in the last changepoint.

The focus of my project is real-time predictions, so I am most
interested in online algorithms.  The technique I will propose
is efficient in the sense that when a new data point arrives, it
requires space and time proportional to $n^2$, where $n$ is the
(estimated) number of data points since the last changepoint.

As for most problems in statistics, there are both Bayesian and
frequentist approaches to changepoint detection.  My approach is
Bayesian in the sense that it is focused on computing probabilities
and distributions rather than estimating parameters or testing
hypotheses.


\subsection{Prediction with changepoints}

The ultimate goal of this project is to use time series data to make
predictions.  For example, imagine that we have observed $n$ values
$x_0, x_1, ... x_{n-1}$, and we want to predict the value of $x_n$.

In the simplest case, we might model the series as a random variable
$X$ with a stationary distribution (and, possibly, autocorrelation
structure).  We could use the observed values to estimate the
parameters of $X$ and then use those parameters to predict the next
value or, more generally, the distribution of the next value.

If we know that the process is non-stationary with abrupt changepoints,
then we would not want to use all of the observed data to generate
the prediction.  For example, if we know that there is a changepoint
at time $i$, then data from before that time should be ignored.

In general, we don't know when changepoints occur, so we have to
infer them from the time series itself.  In that case we usually
don't know with certainty whether a changepoint happened at a given
time, but we might be able to compute a probability.

At this point, there are two choices.  The simpler one is to apply
a threshold to the computed probabilities.  If a changepoint is
sufficiently likely, we assume that it occurred and discard older
data.  Otherwise we assume that there was no changepoint.  The other
choice is to use the changepoint probabilities to generate
predictions.  For example, if we think that there is a 75\% chance
of a changepoint at $i$, the we could generate two predictions,
one that assumes that there was a changepoint, and one that assumes
that there wasn't, and then combine the predictions using the
computed probability as a weight.  My technique uses the latter
approach.

Therefore, the proximate goal of this project is to compute
$p(i^+)$, which I define as the probability that $i$ is the index
of the last (most recent) changepoint.  The superscript $+$ is intended
to distinguish from $p(i)$, which is the probability that $i$
is the index of a changepoint (but not necessarily the last).

\section{The algorithm}

Before I can present my algorithm it will be useful to discuss a
simplified problem.

\subsection{The simple version}

Imagine, as in the previous section, that
we have observed a series of $n$ values.  In addition, assume:

\begin{enumerate}

\item We have a model of the process that allows us to compute
the likelihood of a given series. 

\item A changepoint is equally likely (a priori) to occur at any time.

\item We know that exactly one changepoint occurred during the
observed interval.

\end{enumerate}
%
The first is a reasonable assumption for many applications;
the second is more restrictive, but still reasonable;
the third is not usually true at all, but we will soon be able to
eliminate it.

The nice thing about the third assumption is that it allows
us to formulate a series of hypotheses, $i^+$, that are mutually
exclusive.  Since we know that there is only one changepoint, we
know that exactly one value of $i$ can be the index of the
first changepoint.

It is, therefore, possible to convert the computed likelihoods
(from the first assumption) into probabilities just by dividing
through by the total likelihood.


\subsection{The complicated version}

Usually we don't know how many changepoints occurred, so the
general form of the problem is more complicated.  The kernel
of my algorithm is the following system of equations:

\newcommand{\p}{\ p}

\begin{eqnarray*}
p(i^+) &=& \sum_{j<i} p(i^+ | j^{++}) \p(j^{++}) + p(i^+ | jc) \p(jc)   \\
p(i^{++}) &=& \sum_{k>i} p(i^{++} | k^+) \p(k^+) \\
\end{eqnarray*}
%
with the following definitions:

\begin{itemize}

\item $i^+$ is the hypothesis that $i$ is the index of the last
(most recent) changepoint, and $p(i^+)$ is its probability.

\item $i^{++}$ is the hypothesis that $i$ is the
second-to-last changepoint.

\item $jc$ is the hypothesis that there are fewer than two
changepoints during the observed interval.  The probability
of $jc$ is the complement of the sum of $p(j^{++})$.

\item $p(i^+ | j^{++})$ is the probability that $i$ is the last
changepoint, given that $j$ is the second-to-last.  Computing this
probability reduces to the simplified problem described in the
previous section because if $j$ is the second-to-last changepoint,
we know that there is exactly one changepoint between $j$ and $n$.

\item $p(i^+ | jc)$ is the probability that $i$ is the last
changepoint, given there are fewer than two changepoints in the
interval.  This reduces to the simplified problem described in the
previous section, extended slightly to deal with the
possibility that there are no changepoints.

\item $p(i^{++} | k^+)$ is the probability that $i$ is the
second-to-last changepoint given that $k$ is the last.  If $k$ is a
changepoint, then all data after time $k$ is irrelevant, so $p(i^{++}
| k^+) = p_k(i^+)$, where $p_k$ indicates the probability that was
computed at time $k$.  If we store previous results, we don't
have to compute this value; we can just look it up.

\end{itemize}

At each time step, we have to recompute $p(i^+ | j^{++})$ and $p(i^+ |
jc)$.  Then we could solve the system of equations for $p(i^+)$ and
$p(i^{++})$, but since the result from the previous time step is
likely to be a good starting approximation, it is sufficient (and more
efficient) to perform one iteration of Jacobi's method per time step.


\section{Results}

I have tested an implementation of this algorithm on synthetic data.
Figure 1 shows a time series sampled from a normal distribution with
$\sigma = 1$ and a breakpoint at $i=50$.  Before the breakpoint, the
mean is 0.0; after the breakpoint it is 0.7.  I chose these values to
generate a breakpoint that is detectable but subtle enough that its
computed probability is less than 1.

\begin{figure}[tb]
\centerline{\epsfig{figure=series.eps,width=3.2in}}
\caption{A synthetic time series with computed probabilities $p(i^+)$.}
\label{fig.model}
\end{figure}

The gray dotted line shows the time series data.  The solid black line
shows the computed values of $p(i^+)$ accumulated from right to left.
At $i=60$, the cumulative probability is 0.04, indicating only a 4\%
chance that there is a breakpoint after $i=60$.  At $i=40$, the
cumulative probability is 0.65, so the algorithm indicates that there
is a 61\% chance of a breakpoint in the interval $40 < i < 60$.  This
conclusion is consistent with what a human might estimate looking
at the same data.



\section{Significance}

This project represents a new direction for me.  In my prior work I
have applied statistical techniques to problems in network measurement
and modeling.  This is the first project of mine that I think is a
novel contribution in statistics.  In particular, it is the first
project where I have had a chance to apply my interest in (and slowly
accumulating knowledge of) Bayesian statistics.  As a way of getting
access to researchers and venues in statistics, I am hoping to
collaborate with a statistician, possibly Emmanuel Yashchin at IBM
Research, who has done work that is closest to what I am proposing
\cite{yashchin95estimating}.

If successful, my algorithm is likely to be useful for a variety of
applications in prediction and scheduling.  This project was motivated
by my collaboration with Rich Wolski and John Brevik at UC Santa
Barbara.  Their research group is active in developing middleware for
Grid computing environments; my work is of interest to them for
several projects.

I hope that within the next year this project will generate two
publications: one that presents and evaluates the algorithm, targeted
for {\em Annals of Mathematics Statistics} or a similar venue; the
other that applies the algorithm to a problem in network measurement,
targeted for ACM Sigmetrics, a computer science conference.


\section{Timeline}

I have implemented the algorithm and started testing on synthetic data
sets.  In the next few months, I will hand over my prototype to my
collaborators at UCSB so that they can integrate it into the Network
Weather Service, a system that monitors a wide-area network and makes
predictions based on a variety of statistical techniques
\cite{wolski99network}.  This infrastructure will make it possible to
test the performance of my algorithm in direct comparison with other
techniques.

This will be my primary research project this summer.  I hope to
have at least one paper ready for submission by October, which will
be the deadline for Sigmetrics 2007.

My ability to prepare an additional paper for a statistics venue
depends on whether I can recruit a collaborator with more background
in this area.


\section{Educational Benefits}

So far I have not been able to get students to work with me on this
project.  Few students have enough background in statistics to work in
this area or enough interest to develop the background on their own.
However, this project might generate ``spin-off'' projects that
students could work on.

My work in this area contributes to my classes in a much more direct
way.  I use examples from my work in both Software Systems and
Computational Modeling; in both classes students read papers that are
relevant to my research and do exercises where they work with data I
have collected or analyzed.  For example, in Software Systems, we
model the distribution of process lifetimes and discuss its effect
on distributed scheduling, using data from one of my papers.

My interest in Bayesian statistics has
already led to a module in Computational Modeling; at some point I
might be interested in teaching a class on the topic.

This work will be done primarily on campus, so I will have the
opportunity to talk to faculty and students about it, possibly including
a public presentation.  I spent most of Winter Break in Santa Barbara
working on this project with my colleagues there; I may travel to
visit them this summer, and I am hoping that they will visit me here,
in which case I would ask them to present their work at an
Olin Seminar.


